    "hotspots": "Large Language Models (LLMs) and agentic systems, diffusion models for generative tasks, graph neural networks, multimodal foundation models, retrieval-augmented generation, model distillation and efficient adaptation, code generation and program synthesis, rigorous benchmarking and evaluation frameworks",
    "trends": "Convergence towards autonomous multi-agent architectures with planning and tool-use capabilities; expansion of diffusion models into video, 3D, and scientific domains; integration of retrieval mechanisms for knowledge-intensive tasks; emphasis on parameter-efficient fine-tuning and distillation for deployment; growing focus on human-centered AI, robotics embodiment, and security/privacy in generative systems; standardization of benchmarks across modalities and tasks",
    "future_directions": "Development of world models and generative simulators for decision-making; neurosymbolic integration for reasoning and verifiability; scalable on-device and edge deployment of foundation models; trustworthy AI with robustness, fairness, and privacy guarantees; seamless human-AI collaboration interfaces; unified multimodal architectures handling text, vision, audio, and sensor data; automated benchmark generation and dynamic evaluation",
    "analysis_summary": "The current landscape shows a strong consolidation around large foundation models (LLMs, diffusion) extended into agentic, multimodal, and retrieval-enhanced paradigms, with critical emphasis on efficiency (distillation, quantization), rigorous evaluation, and cross-disciplinary applications spanning vision, language, robotics, HCI, and security. Research is shifting from static model performance to dynamic, interactive, and deployable intelligent systems."
